WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 4.5.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:04.510
Vaults & yield optimisers.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.957
By the end, you'll be able to decide, with

00:00:06.957 --> 00:00:09.972
real numbers, when a vault's fees are actually worth

00:00:09.972 --> 00:00:13.215
paying, compared with running the same strategy yourself.

00:00:14.515 --> 00:00:15.805
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.805 --> 00:00:18.159
What a vault actually does with your deposit,

00:00:18.159 --> 00:00:20.250
and the shares you get back for it.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.093
Its three possible costs: performance fees,

00:00:23.093 --> 00:00:26.430
management fees, and sometimes withdrawal fees.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.789
The extra risk layer a vault adds, on

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:31.635
top of every protocol it uses underneath.

00:00:31.635 --> 00:00:34.951
And a full worked example, using the source material's

00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:37.965
own numbers, at two very different position sizes.

00:00:39.265 --> 00:00:40.760
What a vault actually does.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.277
Here's exactly what a vault does with

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:46.385
the assets you deposit into it.

00:00:46.385 --> 00:00:49.644
It pools your deposit together with everyone else's,

00:00:49.644 --> 00:00:52.657
and runs a specific strategy against that pooled

00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:56.162
total, often harvesting rewards on some regular schedule

00:00:56.162 --> 00:00:59.482
and automatically reinvesting them, so you don't have

00:00:59.482 --> 00:01:01.565
to do that manually yourself.

00:01:01.565 --> 00:01:04.041
In exchange for your deposit, you receive

00:01:04.041 --> 00:01:07.164
shares, representing your proportional claim on that

00:01:07.164 --> 00:01:09.522
pooled position, plus whatever it earns.

00:01:10.822 --> 00:01:13.355
Worth being precise about the actual value a

00:01:13.355 --> 00:01:15.831
vault is selling you, since it isn't really

00:01:15.831 --> 00:01:18.363
the strategy itself, most of which you could

00:01:18.363 --> 00:01:20.577
technically replicate on your own.

00:01:20.577 --> 00:01:24.588
It's automation, and pooled efficiency, specifically around the

00:01:24.588 --> 00:01:28.412
repetitive, gas-costing parts of running that strategy well,

00:01:28.412 --> 00:01:32.347
like harvesting and reinvesting on a disciplined schedule.

00:01:32.347 --> 00:01:35.475
Whether that's worth paying for depends entirely on how

00:01:35.475 --> 00:01:38.882
much those repetitive costs would actually cost you directly.

00:01:40.182 --> 00:01:41.272
Quick check.

00:01:41.272 --> 00:01:42.513
Why do vaults tend to

00:01:42.513 --> 00:01:44.827
suit smaller positions particularly well?

00:01:49.247 --> 00:01:52.495
The answer: they spread the gas costs of harvesting

00:01:52.495 --> 00:01:56.243
and reinvesting across every depositor in the pool, instead

00:01:56.243 --> 00:01:58.617
of each person paying that cost alone.

00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:01.282
Three possible costs.

00:02:02.582 --> 00:02:04.645
Here's exactly what each of these three

00:02:04.645 --> 00:02:06.966
costs actually takes from you, since they're

00:02:06.966 --> 00:02:09.587
structured quite differently from each other.

00:02:09.587 --> 00:02:12.682
A performance fee takes a share specifically of your

00:02:12.682 --> 00:02:16.077
profits, only when the strategy actually makes money.

00:02:16.077 --> 00:02:18.381
A management fee takes a share of your

00:02:18.381 --> 00:02:21.454
total assets, every year, regardless of whether the

00:02:21.454 --> 00:02:24.117
strategy made anything at all that year.

00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:26.552
And some vaults add a withdrawal fee too,

00:02:26.552 --> 00:02:29.857
charged specifically when you actually exit the position.

00:02:31.157 --> 00:02:34.887
Worth being precise about why the management fee specifically

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:38.797
deserves extra attention, compared with the performance fee.

00:02:38.797 --> 00:02:41.848
A performance fee only costs you anything when you're

00:02:41.848 --> 00:02:45.537
actually making money, which softens its sting considerably.

00:02:45.537 --> 00:02:48.289
A management fee keeps taking its share every

00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:50.981
single year, even during a year the strategy

00:02:50.981 --> 00:02:54.331
loses money outright, which is a meaningfully different

00:02:54.331 --> 00:02:55.647
kind of cost to carry.

00:02:56.947 --> 00:03:00.533
Worth being precise about why a withdrawal fee specifically

00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:03.402
exists, on the vaults that charge one, since it

00:03:03.402 --> 00:03:06.272
isn't simply extra profit for its own sake.

00:03:06.272 --> 00:03:10.516
It's usually there to discourage very short-term, in-and-out deposits,

00:03:10.516 --> 00:03:13.743
which can otherwise force the vault to pay harvesting

00:03:13.743 --> 00:03:16.493
gas costs on behalf of a depositor who barely

00:03:16.493 --> 00:03:19.362
stayed long enough to benefit from them, at the

00:03:19.362 --> 00:03:21.932
expense of everyone else still in the pool.

00:03:23.232 --> 00:03:24.322
Quick check.

00:03:24.322 --> 00:03:27.166
Gross twelve percent, a ten percent performance

00:03:27.166 --> 00:03:29.717
fee, and a one percent management fee.

00:03:29.717 --> 00:03:30.967
What's the net return?

00:03:35.387 --> 00:03:37.922
The answer: nine point eight percent.

00:03:37.922 --> 00:03:41.092
Twelve times zero point nine, minus one percent.

00:03:42.392 --> 00:03:43.687
The extra risk layer.

00:03:44.987 --> 00:03:48.044
Here's exactly what risk a vault actually adds,

00:03:48.044 --> 00:03:50.846
on top of whatever you'd already be exposed

00:03:50.846 --> 00:03:54.012
to by using its underlying strategy directly.

00:03:54.012 --> 00:03:57.118
Its own smart contract code can contain a bug,

00:03:57.118 --> 00:04:00.722
entirely separate from any protocol it invests in.

00:04:00.722 --> 00:04:03.713
Its own strategy logic can make a mistake in

00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:07.337
how or when it harvests, reinvests, or rebalances.

00:04:07.337 --> 00:04:10.357
And every single protocol it deposits your funds

00:04:10.357 --> 00:04:13.069
into underneath, all of that risk, is still

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:16.213
fully yours too, completely unremoved by the vault

00:04:16.213 --> 00:04:17.507
sitting on top of it.

00:04:18.807 --> 00:04:21.278
Here's why that extra risk layer isn't

00:04:21.278 --> 00:04:23.621
the same size for every vault, since

00:04:23.621 --> 00:04:26.962
complexity itself is a real, checkable variable.

00:04:26.962 --> 00:04:30.628
A simple vault, harvesting and reinvesting a single,

00:04:30.628 --> 00:04:34.710
well-understood strategy on one protocol, adds a genuinely

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.362
small amount of extra risk on top.

00:04:37.362 --> 00:04:41.200
A complex vault, looping through several protocols, or adding

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.420
its own leverage on top of the underlying strategy,

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.754
adds meaningfully more, since every additional protocol and mechanism

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:51.292
is one more place something can go wrong.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:53.682
Quick check.

00:04:53.682 --> 00:04:56.067
What extra risk does a vault actually add?

00:05:00.487 --> 00:05:02.988
The answer: its own code and strategy,

00:05:02.988 --> 00:05:05.233
on top of every single protocol it

00:05:05.233 --> 00:05:07.542
deposits your funds into underneath.

00:05:08.842 --> 00:05:09.847
Worked example.

00:05:11.147 --> 00:05:13.479
Here's the net calculation first, using

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.762
the source material's own numbers.

00:05:15.762 --> 00:05:17.977
Gross return: ten percent.

00:05:17.977 --> 00:05:20.103
A twenty percent performance fee takes

00:05:20.103 --> 00:05:22.257
two points, leaving eight percent.

00:05:22.257 --> 00:05:24.511
A zero point five percent management

00:05:24.511 --> 00:05:26.577
fee takes another half point.

00:05:26.577 --> 00:05:30.156
Net result: seven point five percent, after both fees.

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:35.198
Now here's the exact same strategy, run yourself instead,

00:05:35.198 --> 00:05:38.359
compounding weekly, at a flat two dollars of gas

00:05:38.359 --> 00:05:42.465
per harvest, compared at two very different position sizes.

00:05:42.465 --> 00:05:45.606
At five thousand dollars, that's one hundred four

00:05:45.606 --> 00:05:47.805
dollars a year in gas, a two point

00:05:47.805 --> 00:05:51.135
zero eight percent drag, considerably worse than the

00:05:51.135 --> 00:05:53.320
vault's own combined fee cost.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.349
At five hundred thousand dollars, that exact same

00:05:56.349 --> 00:05:58.773
one hundred four dollars is only a zero

00:05:58.773 --> 00:06:01.621
point zero two percent drag, while the vault's

00:06:01.621 --> 00:06:03.923
fees still cost two and a half points.

00:06:05.223 --> 00:06:07.596
Put side by side, here's the actual

00:06:07.596 --> 00:06:11.257
comparison at each size, in percentage-point terms.

00:06:11.257 --> 00:06:14.734
At the smaller size, D.I.Y.'s own gas drag of

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:18.662
two point zero eight percent is the bigger cost.

00:06:18.662 --> 00:06:21.716
At the larger size, the vault's own fee drag

00:06:21.716 --> 00:06:25.110
of two point five points becomes the bigger cost,

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:28.707
while D.I.Y.'s gas drag has shrunk to almost nothing.

00:06:30.007 --> 00:06:32.122
Finding the actual break-even point.

00:06:33.422 --> 00:06:36.115
Here's that same one hundred four dollars a

00:06:36.115 --> 00:06:38.380
year in gas, run across a much wider

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.829
range of position sizes, so you can see

00:06:40.829 --> 00:06:43.883
exactly where the crossover actually happens.

00:06:43.883 --> 00:06:46.408
At one thousand dollars, gas drag is over

00:06:46.408 --> 00:06:49.233
ten percent, clearly worse than the vault.

00:06:49.233 --> 00:06:52.349
It keeps falling as size grows, crossing the vault's

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:54.700
own two and a half point fee drag right

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:57.763
around four thousand one hundred sixty dollars.

00:06:57.763 --> 00:07:01.289
Above that point, the vault's fees become the bigger

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:04.483
cost every time; below it, D.I.Y.'s gas drag is.

00:07:05.783 --> 00:07:08.398
Notice what that break-even number actually

00:07:08.398 --> 00:07:10.243
tells you, in plain terms.

00:07:10.243 --> 00:07:13.511
Above roughly four thousand one hundred sixty dollars,

00:07:13.511 --> 00:07:16.184
at this specific gas cost and fee structure,

00:07:16.184 --> 00:07:19.098
running the strategy yourself probably wins.

00:07:19.098 --> 00:07:21.423
Below it, the vault probably wins.

00:07:21.423 --> 00:07:23.962
Change the gas cost, or the vault's own

00:07:23.962 --> 00:07:26.692
fees, and that exact number moves, but the

00:07:26.692 --> 00:07:29.548
method for finding it stays exactly the same.

00:07:30.847 --> 00:07:33.319
Here's how to actually run this comparison

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:35.560
for your own numbers, rather than just

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.872
reusing the ones from this example.

00:07:37.872 --> 00:07:40.854
Estimate your own realistic gas cost per harvest,

00:07:40.854 --> 00:07:43.242
and how often you'd actually do it.

00:07:43.242 --> 00:07:45.850
Divide that annual gas cost by your own

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:49.345
position size, for your own real drag percentage.

00:07:49.345 --> 00:07:51.861
And compare that number directly against the

00:07:51.861 --> 00:07:54.041
vault's own total fee drag, the larger

00:07:54.041 --> 00:07:55.830
one is the one costing you more.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:59.744
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:07:59.744 --> 00:08:02.419
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:08:02.419 --> 00:08:04.725
more than either specific number.

00:08:04.725 --> 00:08:07.407
The exact same vault, with the exact same

00:08:07.407 --> 00:08:10.218
fees, is either clearly worth it or clearly

00:08:10.218 --> 00:08:13.347
not, purely depending on your own position size,

00:08:13.347 --> 00:08:15.583
since gas is a flat cost that only

00:08:15.583 --> 00:08:18.265
a larger position can meaningfully dilute.

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:21.025
Here's your checklist.

00:08:21.025 --> 00:08:23.600
Do it before depositing into any vault.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.155
Compute the actual net A.P.Y., after every fee the

00:08:27.155 --> 00:08:31.080
vault charges, not just its advertised gross number.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.992
Compare that against your own D.I.Y. gas

00:08:33.992 --> 00:08:36.833
drag, at your own actual position size,

00:08:36.833 --> 00:08:39.690
the way the worked example just did.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:43.833
And review every single underlying protocol the vault actually

00:08:43.833 --> 00:08:47.450
deposits into, since that risk is still entirely yours.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:49.995
Let's recap.

00:08:49.995 --> 00:08:52.616
A vault pools deposits, runs a strategy,

00:08:52.616 --> 00:08:55.025
and hands you shares in exchange.

00:08:55.025 --> 00:08:58.151
It can charge a performance fee, a management fee,

00:08:58.151 --> 00:09:02.005
and sometimes a withdrawal fee, each behaving differently.

00:09:02.005 --> 00:09:04.302
It adds its own code and strategy

00:09:04.302 --> 00:09:06.598
risk, on top of every protocol it

00:09:06.598 --> 00:09:09.735
uses underneath, never removing that risk.

00:09:09.735 --> 00:09:12.981
And whether it's worth it comes down to one

00:09:12.981 --> 00:09:17.112
real comparison: its net A.P.Y. against your own D.I.Y.

00:09:17.112 --> 00:09:19.620
gas drag, at your own actual size.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.760
Compute the net APY after every fee, compare

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.096
it against your own gas drag at your

00:09:26.096 --> 00:09:29.110
size, and review every protocol underneath.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:33.695
Next up, Lesson 4.6: airdrops and points, opportunity cost.
